 {"id":"QA-TERMINAL-JAIL-11","status":"pending","priority":"P3","title":"[P3] upgrade cell FAIL is a channel mismatch — tag v1.0.0 exists but terminal-jail was never published to PyPI, so the pip-based upgrade probe cannot install the previous release","cell":"upgrade","detail":"pip install terminal-jail==1.0.0 → 'No matching distribution found' (from versions: none). The project's real install path is editable/install.sh (README: uv sync --dev), not PyPI, so the harness's pip-channel upgrade probe fails by construction and the v1.0.0→HEAD upgrade path stays UNVERIFIED this cycle. Harness-side fix: skip or repoint the upgrade cell for projects with no published distribution; project-side option: publish releases or document that upgrade testing must use git-checkout of the tag. Not a code defect.","reasoning":"QA foreman cycle 2026-09-20 — fresh-system CI pass + chaos on bunker-las-02 (upgrade)","review_notes":"Evidence: qa-logs-2991846/up1.log (pip error), up2.log (editable install of HEAD 1.1.0 succeeded).","source":"terminal-jail-qa lane 2026-09-20-07-26-53","created_at":"2026-09-20T08:16:18.128958+00:00","attempts":0}
-{"status": "pending", "primary_model": "glm-5.3-flash", "primary_provider": "zai-glm-default", "created_at": "2026-09-21T11:23:44+00:00", "attempts": 0, "id": "TJ-GAP-070", "priority": "P1", "title": "[P1] Engine crash inside a user rule file fails the firewall OPEN: bridge catches the exception and emits action=allow with a [bridge-error] reason the wrapper never inspects — one malformed user rule (e.g. priority: not-a-number) silently disables ALL rule protection", "detail": "Verified end-to-end 2026-09-21: with TERMINAL_JAIL_INTERRUPTOR_RULES_DIR pointing at a dir whose only yaml sets priority: not-a-number, `bridge({'command': 'true'})` returns rc=0, action=allow, rule_id=null, reason '[bridge-error] interrupt() raised an exception — fail-open: allowing command' (interruptor_bridge.py:68-71 catches Exception and calls _emit_fail_open), and the full wrapper `standalone/terminal-jail true` exits 0 and RUNS the command with zero warning — enforce mode's documented fail-closed contract does not trigger because the bridge exits 0 with valid JSON. Two wrapper fail-open hazards sit beside it: the verdict parser does `|| echo \"allow\"` on any bridge stdout that is not JSON (standalone/terminal-jail:319), and empty-stdout-with-exit-0 falls through unparsed. Control probe (clean rules dir): builtin-chmod-777-root still blocks — the firewall itself is healthy; the hole is exception-to-allow. DF-TERMINAL-JAIL-6 fixed the INPUT schema boundary (bridge still fails open there, documented); TJ-GAP-063 fixed the BRIDGE-UNAVAILABLE boundary (fails closed); the ENGINE-EXCEPTION boundary was never decided and currently defaults to allow.", "reasoning": "stand-in PM cycle 2026-09-21 (hunter-verified real-use finding; PM re-ran every load-bearing probe before gating) || TICK #306 FOREMAN PREMISE-CHECK (independent, same-day): REPRODUCED as described. With TERMINAL_JAIL_INTERRUPTOR_RULES_DIR pointing at a dir whose only yaml sets `priority: not-a-number`, the bridge on `{\"command\": \"terraform destroy\"}` returns rc=0, action=allow, rule_id=null, reason `[bridge-error] interrupt() raised an exception — fail-open: allowing command`. Control probe (clean rules dir): builtin-chmod-777-root still blocks — the firewall itself is healthy; the hole is exception-to-allow. Note for the fixing tick: loader-boundary crash shapes (malformed YAML, invalid regex, wrong field types) are ALREADY silently skipped with a WARNING per DF-TERMINAL-JAIL-6 — this row's hole is specifically the post-parse processing/evaluation exception path.", "review_notes": "Decide and implement the contract explicitly: (a) preferred — the bridge distinguishes 'policy says allow' from 'engine could not evaluate': on exception emit action=deny (or a dedicated 'error' action) with the [bridge-error] reason so the wrapper's enforce mode blocks, keeping the allow envelope ONLY for malformed stdin/transport where blocking could brick the host shell; validate rules at LOAD time too (rule-pack-tool-style schema pass over rules.d on RuleSet init: refuse the whole file with a loud one-line stderr note, so a typo cannot arm this path). (b) wrapper-side hardening regardless: treat any verdict carrying a non-empty reason starting with [bridge-error] as block in enforce mode, and treat empty/non-JSON bridge stdout as block (never `|| echo allow`). PASS: (1) poison-priority fixture (priority: not-a-number) -> `standalone/terminal-jail true` exits 126 with a block/error box and the payload does not run, enforce mode; (2) the same fixture in warn mode runs with a loud warning line; (3) bridge emitting garbage on stdout -> enforce blocks; (4) all existing suites stay green and the clean-rules control probe still allows/blocks exactly as before (no regression on the DF-6 contract).", "source": "pm-standin-2026-09-21"}
